fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1125", description: some(value: "A carrier-own `neq` is an ACCEPTED override that NOTHING DISPATCHES — make it dispatchable across the index, the resolver and eval, or decide it is not an override point and refuse it. FOUND BY: the WI-755 review, then measured three ways. THE SHAPE: a sort declaring `operation neq(a: C, b: C) -> Bool` and no own `eq`. It is legal and deliberately so — WI-1098/WI-999 accept it as an OVERRIDE, pinned by wi1098_derive_eq_total_test::declaring_neq_on_a_derived_carrier_is_an_override_not_a_capture (tests/include/wi1098_derive_eq_total_test.rs:624), whose comment says in as many words 'a derived provides PartialEq makes an neq declaration an OVERRIDE'. WHAT HONOURS IT: nothing. load.rs build_eq_dispatch_index keys ONLY `anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq` suppliers (EqDispatchIndex::build reads eq_spec/eq_short; candidates() calls carrier_own_op(kb, carrier, self.eq_spec, self.eq_short)), so such a carrier's constructors key no entry; resolve.rs sem_eq_dispatch_target therefore finds no target and value_reaches_eq_override finds nothing buried, and sem_eq_values falls through to outcome 6, the STRUCTURAL verdict — against the carrier's own inequality. eval::builtins is blind identically: builtin_neq is `!semantic_equal(..)` and semantic_equal reads the same index. So the declaration is accepted, documented as an override, and silently ignored by every evaluator. MEASURED (probe over the stdlib + a Color carrier whose ONLY member is `operation neq(a,b) = false`, i.e. nothing unequal so everything is equal): (1) GUARD path, `effects { Boom :- eq(c, Red) }` at risky(Green) — on HEAD before WI-755 the WI-573 blanket gate KEPT Boom; with WI-755's gate removed outright it DROPPED it (a silent wrong answer); WI-755 therefore kept a narrowed floor, typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, and rows neq_only_override_keeps_effect + nested_neq_only_override_keeps_effect pin it. (2) PROOF path, `requires neq(c, Red)` at needy(Green) — PROVES on HEAD too, i.e. this half was ALREADY WRONG before WI-755 and is what this ticket owns: WI-756 covered the proof paths but every one of its fixtures uses an `eq` override, so nothing reached the neq-only shape. The contract-`ensures` and in-body-`proof` sites resolve through the same builtin and are wrong the same way; census them rather than assuming the two named here are all. SCOPE — decide the design question FIRST, because the two answers are opposite: (a) `neq` IS an override point: EqDispatchIndex must key a carrier-own `neq` and carry the POLARITY (functor -> Symbol becomes functor -> (Symbol, is_neq)), sem_eq_dispatch must invert the verdict for a neq target, eval::builtins::semantic_equal must do the identical thing (resolver/interpreter/codegen agreement is the WI-645 invariant), the AmbiguousEqDispatch refusal must treat an eq supplier and a neq supplier for one carrier as a conflict, and cpp codegen needs a look; THEN retire typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, whose doc names this ticket as its owner. Or (b) it is NOT: `neq(a,b) <=> not(eq(a,b))` is a kernel law (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill:28, spec §8.3 'the negation of the dispatched equality'), so a carrier-own `neq` is unexpressible-by-construction and should be a LOAD ERROR beside the existing AmbiguousEqDispatch/check_eq_noneq_exclusive refusals — which means revisiting WI-1098's row above, since that is the decision it recorded. (b) is the smaller change and the one the spec's own law argues for; (a) is what WI-1098's comment currently promises the user. ACCEPTANCE: whichever arm, the neq-only carrier must stop being silently structural on ALL FOUR prove_from_gamma consumers plus eval — drive each, do not infer; the two WI-755 floor rows either flip to assert the dispatch (arm a) or are replaced by a load-refusal row (arm b), and typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq + NEQ_OVERRIDE_SCAN_DEPTH_CAP + carrier_has_undispatchable_neq + value_reaches_undispatchable_neq are deleted either way; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-17T19:28:15Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1125", name: "wi502")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1125", author: "user", content: "SCOPE ADDITION (from WI-755's second review round, all measured). Three sub-cases, not one — a fix must state its answer for each: (1) NEQ-ONLY carrier (own member OR instance-fact binding): nothing dispatches it, and WI-755's floor now suspends the guard route for it (typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, reading spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier over PartialEq AND Eq, all three supply routes — a route-1-only read MISSED a 'fact PartialEq(T: Color, neq: colorNeq)' binding and dropped the effect; pinned by wi573_eq_override_discharge_test::fact_bound_neq_override_keeps_effect). The PROOF routes are still structural for it and that is this ticket's core. (2) BOTH MEMBERS, CONSISTENT (eq = false, neq = true): the resolver dispatches the eq and is RIGHT — 'neq <=> not(eq)' makes eq the authority. WI-755 deliberately does NOT block here, and the review's proposed one-line fix ('block whenever the carrier supplies its own neq') would have regressed it: MEASURED, restoring WI-573's blanket gate fails carrier_supplying_both_members_dispatches_its_eq. Whatever arm this ticket takes must keep that row dropping. (3) BOTH MEMBERS, CONTRADICTORY (eq = false AND neq = false, or eq = true AND neq = true): the carrier states something the kernel law forbids, the resolver silently answers via eq, and NOTHING CHECKS IT — not check_eq_noneq_exclusive (that is Eq vs NonEq), not AmbiguousEqDispatch (that is two eq suppliers). This is a missing LOAD-TIME COHERENCE refusal and it is the cheapest half of this ticket: a carrier supplying both members owes 'neq = not(eq)', and it can be refused at load beside the existing equality refusals regardless of which arm (a)/(b) is chosen for the dispatch question. Consider delivering (3) first — it is independent, small, and turns a silent wrong answer into a diagnostic.", at: "2026-08-17T17:07:44Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1125", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED — arm (b), TOTAL: a carrier-supplied `neq` is a load error (LoadError::CarrierSuppliesNeq, raised by build_eq_dispatch_index via neq_supplier_refusals), through all three supply routes and with or without an own `eq`.\n\nWHY (b) AND NOT (a): the ticket read arm (b) as 'unexpressible-by-construction'; the stronger argument is that spec §8.3 (kernel-language.md:3082) ALREADY said 'an own `neq` member is never consulted', so (b) enforces a recorded decision rather than making a new one. Every evaluator already derives neq from eq -- resolver sem_eq_core inverts one sem_eq_values, eval builtin_neq is !semantic_equal, cpp-gen maps the pair to ==/!= -- so arm (a) would have put a polarity bit on the hot equality path in three evaluators that must agree (WI-645) to dispatch a member the language defines as derived. NO change was needed in resolve.rs, eval, or cpp codegen.\n\nYOUR THREE SUB-CASES, each answered: (1) neq-only -> REFUSED; the silent structural verdict is gone because the program is. (2) both members consistent -> REFUSED, which departs from the feedback's letter. The behaviour you pinned (the eq dispatches, the effect drops, nothing blocks on the presence of an own neq) is preserved and still pinned, by custom_eq_override_dispatches_and_drops on the eq-only carrier; carrier_supplying_both_members_dispatches_its_eq is retired because with no own neq expressible there is nothing left for a gate to key on. (3) contradictory -> UNREPRESENTABLE rather than checked. This is the reason (2) could not stay legal: 'forall a,b. neq(a,b) = not(eq(a,b))' is not decidable at load, so any coherence check would be a syntactic approximation the fixture happens to fall inside. Removing the member is total, and it is the repo's 'make illegal state unrepresentable over check logic'.\n\nDELETED with the shape it guarded: guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, value_reaches_undispatchable_neq, carrier_has_undispatchable_neq, carrier_supplies_neq, NEQ_OVERRIDE_SCAN_DEPTH_CAP (~175 lines), plus the `subst` parameter three call levels deep that only they needed. Net -60 lines outside tests.\n\nMEASURED, two independent back-outs over 3042 anthill-core tests (mutation, not deletion): (i) discard neq_supplier_refusals' result, leaving the sweep running => EXACTLY 7 fail -- the six *_is_refused rows plus wi1098's flipped row -- and nothing else, which is what says the refusal reaches only programs that supply a neq; (ii) make member_operands_are_the_carrier return true => exactly 1 fails, an_abstract_spec_declaring_the_family_is_not_a_carrier_override. Two axes, two back-outs. COST: release CLI, stdlib, min-of-9 each way, build_eq_dispatch_index 228us -> 446us against a ~109ms load (0.2%).\n\nACCEPTANCE, driven not inferred: wi1125_neq_not_an_override_test (15 rows) covers one per supply route, the both-members and contradictory arms, one per prove_from_gamma consumer PLUS eval (each with a load-clean control on the same shape without the member, so 'refused' cannot be satisfied by a fixture too malformed to reach its consumer), and two repair rows driving the eq spelling of the same meaning through the requires consumer and through the interpreter. cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh (35/35 binaries); scaland sbt test 492/492.\n\n/code-review (high) FOUND FOUR, all reproduced, all fixed and pinned: (1) HIGH -- a debug_assert on the binary arity PANICKED the loader on `operation neq()`, which is ordinary parsable source; both asserts removed, a 0-ary member is simply not this spec op. (2) the witness route was unfiltered, so an unrelated neq(a: Int64, b: Int64) on a witness sort refused an innocent carrier; now filtered on the name-matched leg only -- a written `neq = f` binding stays refused as written. (3) the refusal fires on carriers that never claim PartialEq: diagnosis accepted, PROPOSED FIX REJECTED and why is now at the site -- carrier_self_provides_eq cannot be asked in this pass because it runs BEFORE WI-1098's derive_total_eq, so a provides-gate would excuse WI-1098's own headline fixture by an accident of pass order; the consequence is pinned by a row instead. (4) three '§8.7' cross-references actually landed in §8.3; retargeted, and the rule added to §8.7 where the binding forms are documented.\n\nDOCS: kernel-language.md §8.3 (the rule + both shapes) and §8.7 (only `eq` may be bound); stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill beside the law; docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md, whose 'the type route reaches what the structural route cannot' evidence this ticket WITHDRAWS -- the residual was not a capability the structural route lacked but a declaration nothing implemented.", at: "2026-08-17T19:28:05Z")
